<!-- MAIN — text from "Website Text: Client Bill of Rights and Grievances" (Linda Spyres, 2026-09-26) -->
<section class="main">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="doc">

      <h2 id="bill-of-rights" class="first">Client Bill of Rights</h2>
      <p>California law (Health and Safety Code §11857.2, Senate Bill 349) requires substance use disorder treatment providers to make a Client Bill of Rights available to all clients and prospective clients. You do not need to be a client to read it. Every client also receives a written copy at admission, staff explain it in person, and the client signs to confirm they received it.</p>
      <p>Under California law, every client has the right:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>To be treated for the life-threatening, chronic disease of substance use disorder with honesty, respect, and dignity, including privacy in treatment.</li>
        <li>To be informed of all aspects of the treatment recommended, including the option of no treatment, risks of treatment, and expected results.</li>
        <li>To be treated by qualified staff.</li>
        <li>To receive evidence-based treatment.</li>
        <li>To be treated at the same time for co-occurring behavioral health conditions, when medically appropriate.</li>
        <li>To receive an individualized, outcome-driven treatment plan.</li>
        <li>To remain in treatment for as long as the treatment provider is authorized to treat the client.</li>
        <li>To receive support, education, and treatment for their families and loved ones, if the provider is authorized.</li>
        <li>To receive care in a treatment setting that is safe and ethical.</li>
        <li>To be free from mental and physical abuse, exploitation, coercion, and physical restraint.</li>
        <li>To be informed of these rights once enrolled in treatment, as shown by written acknowledgment.</li>
        <li>To be informed of the law regarding complaints, including the address and telephone number of the California Department of Health Care Services (<a href="#state">see below</a>).</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Clients at Sunbright also have the right to confidentiality under federal law (42 CFR Part 2 and HIPAA), freedom from discrimination, language help and disability accommodations at no cost, and freedom to practice their religion, along with the other rights listed in the full document.</p>
      <p>Read the full Client Bill of Rights: <a href="/docs/sunbright-client-bill-of-rights.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Client Bill of Rights (PDF)</a></p>
      <p>How we protect your health information: <a href="/docs/sunbright-notice-of-privacy-practices.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Notice of Privacy Practices (PDF)</a></p>

      <h2 id="grievance">How to File a Grievance</h2>
      <p>Clients, former clients, family members, and authorized representatives may file a grievance. You can:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Talk to any staff member at the facility, in person or by phone at <a href="tel:+15305134294">530.513.4294</a>. Staff will write down your concern for you.</li>
        <li>Download our Grievance Form — <a href="/docs/sunbright-client-grievance-form.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Client Grievance Form (PDF)</a>. You can type in it or print it and write by hand.</li>
        <li>Return the form by handing it to any staff member or the Clinical Director (you may seal it in an envelope marked “Confidential – Clinical Director”), or by mailing it to:
          <p class="addr">Sunbright Recovery Center<br/>Attn: Clinical Director – CONFIDENTIAL<br/>22425 Sunbright Avenue<br/>Red Bluff, CA 96080</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>For your privacy, please do not email grievance forms. Regular email is not secure.</p>
      <p>Representatives: because client records are protected by federal law, we can discuss a grievance with you only if the client has signed a written authorization naming you.</p>

      <h2 id="next">What Happens Next</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We confirm that we received your grievance, in writing, within 5 calendar days.</li>
        <li>The Clinical Director looks into it and sends you a written response within 30 calendar days.</li>
        <li>If you are not satisfied, you may appeal to our Program Director or Executive Director within 10 calendar days of the response.</li>
        <li>No one may punish, discharge, or change a client’s care because a grievance was filed.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="state">Contact the State at Any Time</h2>
      <p>You do not have to use our process first. You may contact the California Department of Health Care Services (DHCS) directly:</p>
      <p class="addr">DHCS Licensing and Certification Division<br/>P.O. Box 997413, MS 2601, Sacramento, CA 95899-7413<br/>Toll-free: <a href="tel:+18776858333">(877) 685-8333</a> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Phone: <a href="tel:+19163222911">(916) 322-2911</a><br/>Email: <a href="mailto:sudcomplaints@dhcs.ca.gov">sudcomplaints@dhcs.ca.gov</a></p>
      <p>Medi-Cal members may also call the member services number on the back of their Medi-Cal card.</p>

      <p class="crisis-line">In crisis? Call or text <a href="tel:988">988</a>, the Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline. In an emergency, call <a href="tel:911">911</a>.</p>

    </article>
